<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - HW9, online version: Stokes' theorem.  The three Stokes       -->
<!-- problems were moved here from Assignment 10 under their old xml:ids     -->
<!-- (ex-hw10o-*), so exercise links and saved answers stay; the Green's     -->
<!-- theorem problems that used to be here are now in Assignment 8           -->
<!-- (hw8-online.ptx).  Auto-graded and randomized: each problem draws        -->
<!-- its constants in <setup> and the answer objects are built from them, so  -->
<!-- every student sees the same problem with different numbers.  Every blank -->
<!-- is graded on its own, so partial credit survives.                        -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Notation follows the text: fluxes are written                           -->
<!--   \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma,                   -->
<!-- circulation is \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}, and the boundary    -->
<!-- curve is called C.                                                       -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Each <solution> is a bare element whose *children* carry                 -->
<!-- component="hw9-solutions"; the element itself is never dropped and it    -->
<!-- carries include-automatic="no".  See the comment at the top of           -->
<!-- source/review1-practice.ptx for why that matters.                        -->
<!-- The last three problems (ex-r4p-*) moved here from Review Problems #4,  -->
<!-- under their old xml:ids; their solutions were rewritten in this file's -->
<!-- pattern (bare <solution include-automatic="no">, children carrying     -->
<!-- component="hw9-solutions").                                            -->
<!-- The section keeps its old xml:id (sec-exercises-hw8-online) so the page  -->
<!-- address and the desk topic stay the same.                               -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-hw8-online" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 9</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Section: <xref ref="section-stokes-theorem" text="title"/>. Work each
      problem, then type your answers into the boxes and have them checked
      here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term> Give numbers as decimals to at
      least three places, with no units and no <m>\pi</m>. A negative answer
      needs its minus sign.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-hw8-online">

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-stokes-hemisphere">
    <title>A Curl Integral Over a Hemisphere</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="rad" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2*a*pi*rad^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m>, where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle <eval obj="a"/>y,\ -<eval obj="a"/>x,\
        yx^3 \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>S</m> is the portion of the sphere of radius
        <m><eval obj="rad"/></m> with <m>z \ge 0</m>, taken with
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        Answer: <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        Curling this field and integrating over the cap is a great deal of
        work for nothing. Stokes' theorem replaces the cap by its boundary
        curve <m>C</m>, the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = R^2</m> in the plane
        <m>z = 0</m>, traversed counterclockwise seen from above to match the
        upward <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>. On <m>C</m> we have <m>z = 0</m>, so
        the third component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> never contributes:
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \oint_C a\,y\, dx - a\,x\, dy .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        With <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle R\cos t,\ R\sin t,\ 0
        \right\rangle</m> the integrand is
        <m>-aR^2\sin^2 t - aR^2\cos^2 t = -aR^2</m>, a constant, so the
        integral is <m>-2\pi aR^2</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        Green's theorem gives the same thing in one step:
        <m>\oint_C a\,y\, dx - a\,x\, dy = \iint_D (-2a)\, dA
        = -2a\pi R^2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-verify-hemisphere">
    <title>Verifying Stokes' Theorem on a Hemisphere</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="rad" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>rad*rad</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>-pi*rad^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify Stokes' theorem for
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle y,\ z,\ x \right\rangle</m> on the
        hemisphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = <eval obj="rsq"/></m>,
        <m>y \ge 0</m>, with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The surface integral
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m> equals
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The circulation
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> around the
        boundary curve <m>C</m> equals
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        First, <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle -1,\ -1,\ -1 \right\rangle</m>, a constant field.
      </p>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        For the surface integral, close the hemisphere with the flat disk
        <m>x^2 + z^2 \le R^2</m> in the plane <m>y = 0</m>. A constant field
        has zero divergence, so the total flux out of that closed surface is
        <m>0</m>: the flux through the hemisphere is minus the flux through
        the disk. The disk's outward normal is <m>-\mathbf{j}</m> and its
        area is <m>\pi R^2</m>, so its flux is
        <m>\left\langle -1, -1, -1 \right\rangle \cdot
        \left(-\mathbf{j}\right)\pi R^2 = \pi R^2</m>, and
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = -\pi R^2 .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        The boundary curve <m>C</m> is the circle of radius <m>R</m> in the
        plane <m>y = 0</m>. Parametrize it by
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle R\cos t,\ 0,\ -R\sin t
        \right\rangle</m>, the direction that matches the outward
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>. Then
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle 0,\ -R\sin t,\ R\cos t
        \right\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = \left\langle -R\sin t,\ 0,\ -R\cos t
        \right\rangle</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_0^{2\pi} -R^2\cos^2 t\, dt = -\pi R^2 ,
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        which is the same number. Stokes' theorem checks out.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-verify-cone">
    <title>Verifying Stokes' Theorem on a Cone</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*a*pi*h^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify Stokes' theorem for
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle -<eval obj="a"/>y,\ <eval obj="a"/>x,\
        -<eval obj="b"/> \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        on the cone <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m>, with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>
        pointing upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The surface integral
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m> equals
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The circulation
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> around the
        boundary curve <m>C</m> equals
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        Here <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ 2a \right\rangle</m>; the constant third
        component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> curls to nothing.
      </p>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        Parametrize the cone by <m>\mathbf{r}(u, \theta)
        = \left\langle u\cos\theta,\ u\sin\theta,\ u \right\rangle</m> with
        <m>0 \le u \le h</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_\theta
        = \left\langle -u\cos\theta,\ -u\sin\theta,\ u \right\rangle ,
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        whose third component is positive, so this is already the upward
        orientation. Only that third component meets the curl, giving
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{h} 2au\, du\, d\theta = 2a\pi h^2 .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        The boundary curve <m>C</m> is the circle of radius <m>h</m> in the
        plane <m>z = h</m>, counterclockwise seen from above:
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle h\cos t,\ h\sin t,\ h
        \right\rangle</m>. Then
        <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{r}'(t)
        = ah^2\sin^2 t + ah^2\cos^2 t = ah^2</m>, so the circulation is
        <m>2a\pi h^2</m> as well.
      </p>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        Notice that the answer never sees <m>b</m>, and that it is the same
        as the curl integral over the flat disk capping the cone: Stokes'
        theorem only cares about the boundary.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>


  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 1.  Verifying Stokes' theorem on a paraboloid  (moved from Review Problems #4, #1)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-verify-paraboloid">
    <title>Verifying Stokes' Theorem on a Paraboloid</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="3" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="u" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="0" max="1" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="d" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>a+(2*u-1)*d</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="kcomp" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*d</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*h*(2*u-1)*d</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Confirm that Stokes' Theorem holds for
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle <eval obj="a"/>\left(y+z\right),\
        <eval obj="b"/>x,\ <eval obj="c"/>yz \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and the surface <m>S</m> that is the portion of the paraboloid
        <m>z = x^2+y^2</m> with <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m>,
        oriented with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing upward and its
        boundary <m>C</m> traversed counterclockwise as seen from above.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of
        <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> is
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="kcomp" width="10"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Give the common value of the two sides,
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \iint_S \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma = </m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="value" width="14"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="kcomp.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))" max="kcomp.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="value.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))" max="value.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        Writing <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> for the three coefficients,
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle cz,\ a,\ b-a \right\rangle ,
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        so the <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component is
        <m>b-a = <eval obj="kcomp"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        <term>The line integral.</term> The plane
        <m>z = <eval obj="h"/></m> meets the paraboloid in the circle
        <m>x^2+y^2 = <eval obj="h"/></m>, so <m>C</m> has radius
        <m>R = \sqrt{<eval obj="h"/>}</m>. With
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle R\cos t,\ R\sin t,\
        <eval obj="h"/> \right\rangle</m>,
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_0^{2\pi} \left(-aR^2\sin^2 t - a<eval obj="h"/>R\sin t
          + bR^2\cos^2 t\right) dt
        = \pi R^2 (b-a) .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        <term>The surface integral.</term> For the graph
        <m>z = g(x,y) = x^2+y^2</m> the upward normal is
        <m>\mathbf{n} = \mathbf{r}_x \times \mathbf{r}_y
        = \left\langle -2x,\ -2y,\ 1 \right\rangle</m>, so on <m>S</m>
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot \mathbf{n}
        = -2cx\left(x^2+y^2\right) - 2ay + (b-a) .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        The first term is odd in <m>x</m> and the second is odd in
        <m>y</m>, so both integrate to zero over the disk
        <m>R</m> of radius <m>\sqrt{<eval obj="h"/>}</m>, leaving
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \iint_S \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = (b-a)\iint_R dx\, dy = (b-a)\,\pi\,<eval obj="h"/> .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        The two sides agree, as <xref ref="eq-stokes"/> promises. Notice
        that <m><eval obj="c"/></m> never reaches the answer: it rides on
        the <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component of the curl, which is killed by
        the symmetry of the disk.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 2.  Circulation of a planar field around two circles  (moved from Review Problems #4, #2)       -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-two-circles-planar">
    <title>Circulation of a Planar Field Around Two Circles</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="3" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="kcomp" context="number">
        <de-expression>b+1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circA" context="number">
        <de-expression>-(b+1)*pi*r^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle <eval obj="a"/>x - <eval obj="b"/>y,\
        x + <eval obj="c"/>y,\ 0 \right\rangle .
      </me>
      <p>
        Use Stokes' Theorem to make the following circulation
        calculations. Each circle <m>C</m> has radius
        <m><eval obj="r"/></m> and is centred at the origin.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of
        <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> is
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="kcomp" width="10"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> For <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, oriented
        clockwise as viewed from the positive <m>z</m>-axis,
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = </m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="circA" width="14"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> For <m>C</m> in the <m>yz</m>-plane, oriented
        clockwise as viewed from the positive <m>x</m>-axis,
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = </m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="14"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="kcomp.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))" max="kcomp.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circA.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))" max="circA.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-0.01" max="zero.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        The field has no <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component and neither component
        involves <m>z</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ 1 - \left(-<eval obj="b"/>\right)
        \right\rangle
        = \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ <eval obj="kcomp"/> \right\rangle ,
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        a constant field pointing along <m>\mathbf{k}</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        <term>Part B.</term> Take <m>S</m> to be the disk the circle
        bounds. Clockwise as seen from the positive <m>z</m>-axis is the
        orientation whose normal points <em>down</em>, so
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = -\mathbf{k}</m> and
        <m>\left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = -<eval obj="kcomp"/></m>. By <xref ref="eq-stokes"/>,
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = -<eval obj="kcomp"/> \iint_S d\sigma
        = -<eval obj="kcomp"/>\,\pi\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^2 .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        <term>Part C.</term> Now <m>S</m> is a disk in the
        <m>yz</m>-plane, whose normal points along <m>\mathbf{i}</m>. The
        curl has no <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component, so
        <m>\left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = 0</m> at every point and the circulation is <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        The coefficients <m><eval obj="a"/></m> and
        <m><eval obj="c"/></m> never appear: they multiply <m>x</m> in
        the <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component and <m>y</m> in the
        <m>\mathbf{j}</m>-component, and neither survives the curl.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 3.  A given curl around two circles  (moved from Review Problems #4, #3)                        -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-curl-two-circles">
    <title>Circulation From a Given Curl</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circA" context="number">
        <de-expression>c*pi*r^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circB" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*r^4/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose
      </p>
      <me>
        \nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle 0,\ x^2+z^2,\ <eval obj="c"/> \right\rangle .
      </me>
      <p>
        Find <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>,
        where <m>C</m> is a circle of radius <m><eval obj="r"/></m>
        centred at the origin, in each of the following positions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, oriented
        counterclockwise as viewed from above:
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="circA" width="14"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> <m>C</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane, oriented
        counterclockwise as viewed from the positive <m>y</m>-axis:
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="circB" width="14"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circA.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))" max="circA.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circB.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circB.value({})))" max="circB.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circB.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        <term>Part A.</term> Let <m>S</m> be the disk the circle bounds in
        the <m>xy</m>-plane. Counterclockwise seen from above pairs with
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{k}</m>, so only the
        <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of the curl contributes and it is the
        constant <m><eval obj="c"/></m>. By <xref ref="eq-stokes"/>,
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \iint_S \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot
          \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = <eval obj="c"/> \cdot \left(\text{area of } S\right)
        = <eval obj="c"/>\,\pi\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^2 .
      </me>
      <p component="hw9-solutions">
        <term>Part B.</term> Now <m>S</m> is the disk in the
        <m>xz</m>-plane, with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{j}</m> and
        <m>d\sigma = dx\, dz</m>, so only the
        <m>\mathbf{j}</m>-component contributes. In polar coordinates on
        that disk, with <m>x^2+z^2 = \rho^2</m>,
      </p>
      <me component="hw9-solutions">
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \iint_S \left(x^2+z^2\right) dx\, dz
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{<eval obj="r"/>} \rho^2\,\rho
          \, d\rho\, d\theta
        = \frac{\pi \left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^4}{2} .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
